\section{Relationship to the earlier experiments}
Two earlier executions of this benchmark exist. The first used three source subsamples, two downstream learners and eight arms, with no harness controls and no augmentation control. The second added harness controls and a width-matched noise control but still averaged weighting over learners that cannot respond to it, and its augmentation conclusion rested on a contrast that the structure-preserving control in this study shows to be the wrong one. It is retained in Online Resource 2. Restricting the present execution to the seeds and learners that earlier run also used isolates the effect of the wider grid from the effect of the added arms. @@identity_statement@@ Both restricted and full estimates are reported in the accompanying change report.

@@sourcecap_statement@@ Earlier local bug-history experiments remain supplementary and are never pooled with these results.

\section{Discussion and implications}
\subsection{What counts as evidence of attribution utility}
An improvement over an untreated learner is not evidence that an explanation supplied information, because the operation that applies the explanation also changes scale, dimensionality or channel. The controls used here are cheap: a constant multiplier, a permuted weight vector, an equal-sized random subset, and uninformative columns of matched width. Reporting them alongside the treatment costs one extra run per arm and changes what the comparison can support. We would encourage their use whether or not the resulting effect is small.

\subsection{Why harness controls matter for small effects}
A near-zero difference is uninformative unless the evaluation could have produced something else. The featureless reference and the label permutation establish the lower end of the scale, and the adaptation comparator shows a representation change of documented type moving the same measurement. This is a weaker statement than a power analysis and we do not present it as one: it bounds the kind of effect the design registers, not the smallest effect it could resolve.

\subsection{Scope}
These are descriptive results on one curated collection of seven Java project families with one label construction and one metric schema. They constrain claims about the tested implementations on this benchmark. They do not establish that attribution reuse is useless in general, that other explainers behave the same way, or that a different teacher, budget or feature family would give the same answer.

\section{Threats to validity}
\textbf{Construct validity.} Affected-release labels depend on issue-report accuracy and retrospective linking. The allowlist removes outcome columns and process histories but does not certify every upstream measurement. Static code metrics are proxies for program properties. ROC-AUC, average precision and macro F1 answer different questions and none measures developer utility directly.

\textbf{Internal validity.} The code records transformations, folds, choices and predictions, and validators check their consistency; analysis stops on a failure rather than dropping a cell. Cross-fitting reduces in-sample teacher leakage and source-group tuning excludes the target. Narrow candidate grids, a 400-row source cap, defaults outside those grids and shared random draws nevertheless condition the result. The fourth downstream learner was selected on measured runtime before any result of this execution existed, and that decision is recorded in the protocol. The 54 static metrics are not transformed for skew before fitting the tree and boosting learners. Transformation choice rarely changes defect-model conclusions except for logistic regression \citep{ref15}, which partially, not fully, mitigates the concern that the scale-sensitive learners' small positive weighting effect could interact with untransformed skew rather than with the attribution weights themselves. A post-hoc check of the stored tuning choices, requiring no new model fit, finds the two regimes select the same learner ranking for a given comparison with a median Spearman correlation of 0.85 across the four headline contrasts, and the independently tuned candidate is stable across the 140 checkpoints for every arm with real signal (median modal share 0.97--0.99); the two arms with no genuine source signal, \texttt{coral} and \texttt{permuted\_original}, are the only ones where the tuned choice is close to a coin flip (modal share 0.57--0.58), which is an independent sanity check that the harness is tracking real structure rather than an artefact of the search procedure.

\textbf{Statistical validity.} Project rotations share training projects and repeats share observations, so teacher, learner and seed cells cannot multiply the effective project count. Ten repetitions bound source-sample variability better than three but do not make targets independent. Rare outcomes, including the small defective count in one project, make individual target scores sensitive to particular files. Target ranges and leave-one-target-out ranges are descriptive; neither is a confidence interval or an equivalence bound. No post hoc power or population significance claim is made on the real cross-project targets, because they share source projects and a formal test would overstate the effective sample size. Where independence genuinely holds -- the synthetic positive-control benchmark, whose rows are independently generated per seed -- a two one-sided equivalence test against a predeclared 0.02 ROC-AUC margin finds the weighting comparisons formally equivalent to that margin at every signal level tested, and the selection comparison equivalent at low and moderate signal but not at the highest level, which is the behaviour a test with real power should show rather than a degenerate one that always declares equivalence. A sealed-mapping mechanical re-verification, following a blind-analysis recommendation for defect-prediction studies \citep{ref14}, applied a fixed classification rule to the three headline comparisons under randomly assigned opaque codes before the mapping was unsealed; all three landed in the same magnitude class under the blind rule, consistent with the qualitative characterisation given in the text.

\textbf{External validity.} External here means new to this repository's earlier experiment, not a probability sample of industrial software. The families come from one curated JIRA collection and some share ecosystems and practices. Different languages, newer systems, other explainers, other feature families and chronological deployment settings remain untested.

\textbf{Researcher degrees of freedom.} This extension was designed after earlier results were known. Its protocol and fingerprints record what was fixed before new execution but are not a public preregistration. All completed arms and both tuning regimes are retained, so no favourable comparison is selectively presented. Two further sensitivities -- reducing the 54 metrics to one representative per correlated cluster, and injecting graded source-label noise -- were frozen in a written specification, including numeric decision thresholds set from the results already reported here, before either was executed; that specification is available alongside this manuscript.

\textbf{Predeclared sensitivity outcomes.} Both frozen sensitivities have since been executed. Injecting graded source-label noise (5\%, 10\% and 20\% of source labels flipped per project, independently, without preserving class counts) leaves every one of the nine headline comparisons sign-stable and within the predeclared 0.02 ROC-AUC ceiling at every noise level, while the untreated learner's own ROC-AUC degrades smoothly from 0.775 to 0.730 -- far short of the 0.245-point collapse the harness's own label-permutation control produces, confirming the noise injection is a partial, not total, corruption. The small weighting and selection effects reported above are therefore not an artefact of assuming clean source labels. Reducing the 54 metrics to the 20 representatives selected in advance by lowest variance-inflation factor within each correlated cluster gives a different picture for selection: the \texttt{select\_shap} contrast against \texttt{select\_random} rises from 0.0030 to 0.0196 ROC-AUC, sign-stable under leave-one-target-out (0.0176 to 0.0214) and roughly three times the effect size that a genuinely uninformative selection rule produces on this benchmark's own zero-signal reference. Collinearity among the 54 metrics was therefore suppressing part of a real selection effect, exactly the failure mode the preregistration was written to detect; the effect reported in the main analysis should be read as a lower bound conditional on the correlated 54-feature representation, not as the full magnitude attributable to attribution-based selection. The weighting contrast is materially unchanged against its uniform-multiplier control under decorrelation (0.0007 against 0.0008) but crosses zero against its shuffled-multiplier control (-0.00004 against 0.0003); both values are small enough relative to the harness's own noise floor that we treat this as inconclusive rather than as a reversal.

\textbf{Attribution-mechanism generalization.} Every result above uses SHAP computed from a tree-based teacher. To test whether the weighting and selection effects are specific to that mechanism, we reran the same weighting and selection arms with the attribution source replaced: a logistic-regression teacher explained with \texttt{shap.LinearExplainer} (interventional feature perturbation) instead of any tree model. The weighting contrast replicates closely under this substitution (0.0006 against 0.0008 for the uniform-multiplier control, 0.0004 against 0.0003 for the shuffled-multiplier control), sign-stable under leave-one-target-out in both cases. The selection contrast does not replicate as robustly: it falls from 0.0030 to 0.0004 and its leave-one-target-out range crosses zero (-0.0008 to 0.0072), where the tree-teacher estimate does not. A second, independent substitution holds the teacher model fixed at the study's own RandomForest and instead replaces only the attribution algorithm, computing LIME explanations in place of TreeExplainer SHAP for the same weighting and selection arms; BreakDown was not implemented alongside LIME because no maintained Python package for it was installable in this environment. Under this substitution both weighting and selection replicate closely: 0.0005 against 0.0008 for the uniform-multiplier control, 0.0003 against 0.0003 for the shuffled-multiplier control, and, unlike the linear-teacher substitution, selection also replicates at 0.0026 against 0.0030, sign-stable under leave-one-target-out (0.0013 to 0.0042) over a range that closely matches the tree-teacher SHAP estimate (0.0018 to 0.0042). Together the two substitutions disentangle two design choices the original single-teacher, single-explainer design could not separate: changing only the attribution algorithm (SHAP to LIME) while holding the teacher model fixed leaves both weighting and selection intact, whereas changing only the teacher model family (tree to linear) while holding the attribution family fixed is what makes selection fragile. The selection effect's sensitivity is therefore better attributed to the teacher model family than to the choice of attribution algorithm, a distinction future selection-based transfer studies should treat as two separate design variables rather than one.

